::ENG1_U05_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.V.02] Sentence: The class will reconstruct the timeline after finding new evidence. What does reconstruct most likely mean?
{
=A. build again #Correct. The prefix re- means again, and construct means build, so reconstruct means build again.
~B. read quickly #Incorrect. The word parts do not connect to reading or speed in this sentence.
~C. argue loudly #Incorrect. Nothing in the root construct or prefix re- suggests arguing.
~D. remove completely #Incorrect. Reconstruct means to build again, not to take something away.
}

::ENG1_U05_L02_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.V.02] Word family: revise, revision, revising. What shared meaning connects these words?
{
~A. hiding information #Incorrect. The word family does not suggest hiding; it connects to looking again at work.
=B. looking again to improve #Correct. Revise and revision both connect to reviewing work again and making it better.
~C. speaking in public #Incorrect. These words are about improving work, not giving an oral presentation.
~D. proving a claim #Incorrect. Revision may improve a claim, but the shared meaning is looking again and improving.
}

::ENG1_U05_L02_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.V.02] Sentence: The source seemed credible because it used recent data and named its author. What does credible most likely mean?
{
~A. difficult to read #Incorrect. The sentence focuses on trust signals, not reading difficulty.
~B. copied from another place #Incorrect. The sentence gives signs of reliability, not copying.
=C. able to be believed #Correct. Credible connects to trust or belief, and the sentence gives reasons to trust the source.
~D. full of pictures #Incorrect. Pictures are not mentioned and do not explain the word credible.
}

::ENG1_U05_L02_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.V.02] Word family: predict, prediction, predictable. Which word is a noun?
{
~A. predict #Incorrect. Predict is usually a verb because it names the action of saying what may happen.
~B. predictable #Incorrect. Predictable is an adjective because it describes something that can be expected.
~C. predicting #Incorrect. Predicting can act as a verb form or gerund, but it is not the best noun in this set.
=D. prediction #Correct. Prediction is a noun because it names the idea or statement about what may happen.
}

::ENG1_U05_L02_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Sentence: Constructive feedback helped the writer improve the paragraph. Which paraphrase best keeps the meaning of constructive?
{
~A. Angry feedback made the paragraph confusing. #Incorrect. Constructive does not mean angry, and the meaning is reversed.
=B. Helpful feedback supported improvement in the paragraph. #Correct. Constructive means useful for building or improving something.
~C. Silent feedback removed the paragraph. #Incorrect. Nothing in constructive suggests silence or removal.
~D. Random feedback replaced the paragraph. #Incorrect. Constructive feedback is purposeful and helpful, not random.
}
